1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,856
In November 2020, four hundred and forty million

2
00:00:03,856 --> 00:00:07,021
dollars arrived at a London finance firm.

3
00:00:07,021 --> 00:00:10,342
It was meant to repay a Credit Suisse fund.

4
00:00:10,342 --> 00:00:12,040
The fund never got it.

5
00:00:12,716 --> 00:00:17,670
Four months later, the firm, Greensill Capital, was in administration,

6
00:00:17,670 --> 00:00:21,563
and ten billion dollars of investors' money was frozen.

7
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:24,794
Its founder, Lex Greensill, had been a

8
00:00:24,794 --> 00:00:27,874
government adviser with a Downing Street pass.

9
00:00:27,874 --> 00:00:31,825
A former prime minister lobbied the Treasury on its behalf.

10
00:00:32,483 --> 00:00:35,766
In June 2026, five years on, the official

11
00:00:35,766 --> 00:00:39,610
record finally said what happened to that money.

12
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:41,772
We'll get to that document.

13
00:00:41,772 --> 00:00:43,133
First, the pitch.

14
00:00:43,816 --> 00:00:44,863
Chapter one.

15
00:00:44,863 --> 00:00:45,736
The pitch.

16
00:00:46,183 --> 00:00:48,502
Here is the idea Greensill sold.

17
00:00:48,502 --> 00:00:52,561
A big company owes its suppliers money, but pays slowly.

18
00:00:52,561 --> 00:00:58,215
A financier pays the suppliers early, and collects from the big company later.

19
00:00:58,816 --> 00:01:02,069
Done with real invoices from solid customers,

20
00:01:02,069 --> 00:01:04,888
it is ordinary, low-risk trade finance.

21
00:01:05,550 --> 00:01:11,864
Lex Greensill described his own business to MPs in May 2021.

22
00:01:12,450 --> 00:01:14,783
He told them: "Our business was all

23
00:01:14,783 --> 00:01:18,250
about financing receivables and future receivables."

24
00:01:18,916 --> 00:01:22,313
Future receivables are money a company hopes to be

25
00:01:22,313 --> 00:01:25,302
owed, from sales that have not happened yet.

26
00:01:25,302 --> 00:01:27,272
Hold on to that word, future.

27
00:01:27,272 --> 00:01:28,427
It matters later.

28
00:01:29,083 --> 00:01:30,395
And it grew fast.

29
00:01:30,395 --> 00:01:36,185
SoftBank's Vision Fund invested one and a half billion dollars in 2019, and

30
00:01:36,185 --> 00:01:42,360
Greensill reported revenue of four hundred and twenty million dollars that year.

31
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:44,037
Chapter two.

32
00:01:44,037 --> 00:01:45,930
Friends in high places.

33
00:01:46,383 --> 00:01:51,123
Lex Greensill's ties to government began in 2011, when he

34
00:01:51,123 --> 00:01:56,113
was brought in as an unpaid adviser on supply-chain finance.

35
00:01:56,783 --> 00:02:01,387
A government-commissioned review by the lawyer Nigel Boardman later

36
00:02:01,387 --> 00:02:04,822
found he had Cabinet Office and Number Ten passes,

37
00:02:04,822 --> 00:02:08,464
business cards, a government laptop and a BlackBerry.

38
00:02:09,216 --> 00:02:12,551
The review wrote: "It is unclear why Mr Greensill

39
00:02:12,551 --> 00:02:15,953
was permitted to remain an adviser to government."

40
00:02:16,616 --> 00:02:20,063
In 2020, as Covid hit, David Cameron, by then

41
00:02:20,063 --> 00:02:25,119
working for Greensill, contacted Treasury ministers and officials.

42
00:02:25,119 --> 00:02:27,877
He wanted Greensill to get access to

43
00:02:27,877 --> 00:02:31,247
an emergency Bank of England lending scheme.

44
00:02:32,016 --> 00:02:33,767
The Treasury said no.

45
00:02:33,767 --> 00:02:38,602
A senior Treasury official, Charles Roxburgh, told MPs the

46
00:02:38,602 --> 00:02:42,770
application was rejected at the end of March 2020.

47
00:02:43,450 --> 00:02:47,866
The Treasury Committee later found Mr Cameron's approach showed

48
00:02:47,866 --> 00:02:51,090
"a significant lack of judgement on his part".

49
00:02:51,090 --> 00:02:56,627
It said ministers and officials behaved with "complete and absolute integrity".

50
00:02:57,283 --> 00:03:01,611
And Boardman found Mr Cameron had, on occasion, understated

51
00:03:01,611 --> 00:03:05,572
the nature of his relationship with Greensill Capital.

52
00:03:06,250 --> 00:03:07,350
Chapter three.

53
00:03:07,350 --> 00:03:09,002
Ten billion in funds.

54
00:03:09,450 --> 00:03:12,439
Greensill needed someone to buy its finance.

55
00:03:12,439 --> 00:03:14,954
Its biggest outlet was Credit Suisse.

56
00:03:15,550 --> 00:03:19,224
Greensill packaged the claims into securities and

57
00:03:19,224 --> 00:03:22,599
transferred them to four Credit Suisse funds.

58
00:03:22,599 --> 00:03:27,699
By March 2021, clients had put around ten billion dollars into them.

59
00:03:28,383 --> 00:03:33,695
Switzerland's regulator, FINMA, found the bank relied heavily on Greensill to

60
00:03:33,695 --> 00:03:37,765
select the claims and to arrange the insurance behind them.

61
00:03:38,450 --> 00:03:40,327
Then the portfolio changed.

62
00:03:40,327 --> 00:03:44,082
FINMA found Greensill later transferred future claims,

63
00:03:44,082 --> 00:03:47,837
financing some companies of doubtful creditworthiness.

64
00:03:47,837 --> 00:03:51,244
Credit Suisse, at first, did not grasp the shift.

65
00:03:51,916 --> 00:03:57,631
Inside the bank, a risk manager advised against a bridging loan to Greensill.

66
00:03:57,631 --> 00:04:00,748
FINMA says a senior manager overruled him.

67
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:04,925
FINMA's verdict, in February 2023, was that

68
00:04:04,925 --> 00:04:10,162
Credit Suisse "seriously breached its supervisory obligations".

69
00:04:10,850 --> 00:04:11,824
Chapter four.

70
00:04:11,824 --> 00:04:13,324
The insurance clock.

71
00:04:13,783 --> 00:04:17,380
Much of the structure rested on one thing: trade credit

72
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:20,715
insurance, which pays out if borrowers fail to pay.

73
00:04:21,383 --> 00:04:28,396
Court documents in Australia showed four point six billion dollars of policies, covering

74
00:04:28,396 --> 00:04:33,655
some forty clients, were due to expire on the first of March 2021.

75
00:04:34,350 --> 00:04:38,753
The insurer, part of the Tokio Marine group, had written

76
00:04:38,753 --> 00:04:42,527
in September 2020 that it did not wish to renew.

77
00:04:43,216 --> 00:04:48,097
Greensill asked the Supreme Court of New South Wales to force a renewal.

78
00:04:48,097 --> 00:04:52,300
It warned that more than fifty thousand jobs could be at risk.

79
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:53,520
The court refused.

80
00:04:54,116 --> 00:04:55,242
Chapter five.

81
00:04:55,242 --> 00:04:56,975
Seven days in March.

82
00:04:57,416 --> 00:04:59,507
What followed took about a week.

83
00:05:00,116 --> 00:05:05,021
On the third of March, Germany's regulator BaFin closed Greensill's

84
00:05:05,021 --> 00:05:10,219
German bank to customers, citing an imminent risk of over-indebtedness.

85
00:05:10,883 --> 00:05:15,856
BaFin said a forensic audit found the bank could not provide evidence

86
00:05:15,856 --> 00:05:20,901
that receivables it had bought from the GFG Alliance actually existed.

87
00:05:21,583 --> 00:05:26,973
GFG is the steel and metals group of the businessman Sanjeev Gupta.

88
00:05:26,973 --> 00:05:31,559
Britain's Serious Fraud Office opened an investigation in

89
00:05:31,559 --> 00:05:36,467
May 2021, covering its financing arrangements with Greensill.

90
00:05:36,467 --> 00:05:39,444
Its case page still lists it as open.

91
00:05:40,116 --> 00:05:45,665
On the eighth of March, Greensill Capital's UK companies entered administration.

92
00:05:45,665 --> 00:05:49,272
The Greensill group's combined liabilities were more

93
00:05:49,272 --> 00:05:51,631
than one point six billion pounds.

94
00:05:52,316 --> 00:05:55,542
Earlier that month, Credit Suisse had closed its

95
00:05:55,542 --> 00:05:58,969
four funds, with around ten billion dollars inside.

96
00:05:59,649 --> 00:06:00,516
Chapter six.

97
00:06:00,516 --> 00:06:03,041
The four hundred and forty million.

98
00:06:03,483 --> 00:06:06,051
Now back to the money from the start.

99
00:06:06,051 --> 00:06:09,036
One borrower in the Credit Suisse funds was

100
00:06:09,036 --> 00:06:13,269
Katerra, an American construction company backed by SoftBank.

101
00:06:13,949 --> 00:06:18,611
According to the Insolvency Service, in late 2020 Lex Greensill

102
00:06:18,611 --> 00:06:23,643
caused his companies to enter transactions with Katerra that removed

103
00:06:23,643 --> 00:06:27,860
legal protections from a Credit Suisse fund's investment.

104
00:06:28,616 --> 00:06:33,657
In November 2020, four hundred and forty million dollars came in.

105
00:06:33,657 --> 00:06:39,240
SoftBank had provided it for buying back Katerra notes held by the fund.

106
00:06:39,849 --> 00:06:43,916
Instead, the Insolvency Service says, Mr Greensill caused or

107
00:06:43,916 --> 00:06:46,695
allowed it to be used for other purposes.

108
00:06:46,695 --> 00:06:48,051
The notes defaulted.

109
00:06:48,051 --> 00:06:51,643
The fund lost four hundred and forty million dollars.

110
00:06:52,316 --> 00:06:56,481
Credit Suisse sued SoftBank in London to recover it.

111
00:06:56,481 --> 00:07:01,126
In October 2025 the High Court rejected the claim, finding

112
00:07:01,126 --> 00:07:06,252
SoftBank believed in good faith the money would pay noteholders.

113
00:07:06,916 --> 00:07:11,106
Then, on the second of June 2026, days before a six-week

114
00:07:11,106 --> 00:07:17,092
trial, the government accepted a disqualification undertaking from Mr Greensill.

115
00:07:17,092 --> 00:07:21,207
The government had sought a ban of up to fifteen years.

116
00:07:21,207 --> 00:07:22,479
He accepted nine.

117
00:07:23,149 --> 00:07:27,085
An undertaking means he does not dispute certain

118
00:07:27,085 --> 00:07:31,185
facts, for the purposes of those proceedings only.

119
00:07:31,185 --> 00:07:33,810
It is not a criminal conviction.

120
00:07:33,810 --> 00:07:36,187
The ban runs until June 2035.

121
00:07:36,883 --> 00:07:38,129
Chapter seven.

122
00:07:38,129 --> 00:07:38,931
Who paid.

123
00:07:39,383 --> 00:07:44,776
The fund investors, around twelve hundred wealthy clients, waited years.

124
00:07:44,776 --> 00:07:49,420
By mid-2024 more than seven billion dollars had been returned,

125
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:52,940
with nearly two and a half billion still stuck.

126
00:07:53,616 --> 00:07:59,577
UBS, which had taken over Credit Suisse, set aside about nine hundred million

127
00:07:59,577 --> 00:08:04,841
dollars to buy investors out at ninety per cent of the funds' value.

128
00:08:04,841 --> 00:08:09,795
Credit Suisse expected the wind-down to run until at least 2031.

129
00:08:10,483 --> 00:08:12,929
British taxpayers were exposed too.

130
00:08:12,929 --> 00:08:18,451
Greensill made seven government-backed Covid loans, totalling three hundred and

131
00:08:18,451 --> 00:08:22,296
fifty million pounds, to companies in the GFG Alliance.

132
00:08:22,949 --> 00:08:26,952
The scheme had a limit of fifty million pounds per borrower.

133
00:08:26,952 --> 00:08:30,355
The National Audit Office said taxpayers could lose

134
00:08:30,355 --> 00:08:33,824
almost three hundred and thirty-five million pounds.

135
00:08:33,824 --> 00:08:38,627
Greensill, through its administrators, denies lending outside the rules.

136
00:08:39,283 --> 00:08:42,211
Lex Greensill told MPs: "I bear complete

137
00:08:42,211 --> 00:08:46,164
responsibility for the collapse of Greensill Capital."

138
00:08:46,850 --> 00:08:47,725
Our analysis.

139
00:08:47,725 --> 00:08:50,755
The official findings point to three lessons,

140
00:08:50,755 --> 00:08:53,246
and they apply well beyond Greensill.

141
00:08:53,750 --> 00:08:54,057
One.

142
00:08:54,057 --> 00:08:59,210
Supply-chain finance is only as safe as what is actually inside it.

143
00:08:59,210 --> 00:09:02,517
FINMA found future claims on weak borrowers

144
00:09:02,517 --> 00:09:06,131
sitting where investors expected trade finance.

145
00:09:06,816 --> 00:09:07,100
Two.

146
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:09,443
One letter can be the whole risk.

147
00:09:09,443 --> 00:09:12,923
The insurer said in September it would not renew.

148
00:09:12,923 --> 00:09:16,544
The firm lasted about a week after the cover ended.

149
00:09:17,150 --> 00:09:17,564
Three.

150
00:09:17,564 --> 00:09:19,222
Access is not oversight.

151
00:09:19,222 --> 00:09:23,506
Passes, phone numbers and a former prime minister did not stop

152
00:09:23,506 --> 00:09:27,030
the Treasury, a court or an insurer from saying no.

153
00:09:27,030 --> 00:09:29,518
They just made the fall more public.

154
00:09:30,183 --> 00:09:35,060
If this sounded familiar, watch our First Brands episode: another company

155
00:09:35,060 --> 00:09:39,737
where lenders discovered too late what their finance really rested on.

156
00:09:40,416 --> 00:09:45,141
Next on Filed Under Failure: FTX, from exchange to courtroom.

157
00:09:45,141 --> 00:09:48,395
